# External Code and Licensing

Code and prose from outside this repository arrive with a license attached, whether or not it is visible.

## Limits when quoting

- Quote at most **one short passage per source**, and keep it under roughly 15 words, in quotation marks with the source named. Longer excerpts belong behind a link, not in the file.
- Never reproduce a complete work — a full file, full docs page, article, or example project — even if it is small.
- Never reproduce song lyrics, book passages, or paywalled content in any form.
- Summaries must be substantially shorter than and different from the source. Do not rebuild a source by accumulating excerpts across several edits.

## Before pasting third-party code

- Identify the license. Unknown license means do not paste it.
- Check compatibility with this project's license before copying anything from a GPL/AGPL/SSPL/CC-BY-SA/proprietary source into a permissively licensed codebase, or vice versa.
- Preserve required copyright headers and attribution notices verbatim. Stripping them is a license violation, not a cleanup.
- Prefer depending on the package over vendoring its source. If vendoring is unavoidable, isolate it in one directory and record its origin, version, and license there.

## Snippets from answers and search results

- Treat code from blogs, Stack Overflow, forums, model output, and AI assistants as third-party code: verify it against this project's conventions and rules before it lands.
- Never copy a block whose behavior you cannot explain line by line.

## Self-check before finishing

Review the diff for any block you did not write: confirm its license permits this use, its attribution
is intact, and no quoted passage exceeds the limits above.
